    q: 'Is there a selective MC1R agonist?',
    a: 'As a laboratory tool, yes — BMS-470539 is a small molecule that activates MC1R and largely leaves the other four receptors alone. But it was built to study inflammation, not to make pigment, and no selective MC1R agonist has been developed as an approved drug. The MC1R agonists people actually use or buy — afamelanotide, dersimelagon, the grey-market Melanotan II — are all broad, hitting several melanocortin receptors at once.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is there no clean, selective tanning drug?',
    a: 'Two reasons stack up. First, the five melanocortin receptors are so alike that a molecule shaped to switch on the pigment receptor MC1R easily touches the others, so selectivity has to be painstakingly engineered. Second, there is no approved medical reason to build one: the single approved MC1R agonist, afamelanotide, treats a rare light-sensitivity disease, and cosmetic tanning is not a pathway regulators license drugs down — so the work has never been funded.',
  },
