    q: 'Can you get the tan without the side effects?',
    a: 'Not reliably with today’s broad agonists. Because the same His-Phe-Arg-Trp core that opens MC1R also opens MC4R, a systemic melanocortin that darkens skin tends to bring nausea, flushing and other MC4R effects with it — they are the same molecule acting at different receptors, not separable dial settings.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does “selective” mean for a receptor drug?',
    a: 'A selective agonist switches on one receptor subtype far more strongly than the others. The natural signal α-MSH is the opposite — non-selective, activating four of the five melanocortin receptors. Selectivity has to be engineered in, and it is hard here: even setmelanotide, developed for MC4R, grips the pigment receptor MC1R almost as tightly as its intended target.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Melanotan II selective for MC1R?',
    a: 'No. Both Melanotan I (afamelanotide) and Melanotan II are broad melanocortin agonists that activate several receptors, not MC1R alone. That breadth is exactly why Melanotan II’s documented effects include nausea, facial flushing and spontaneous erections alongside the tan — the erections and nausea are MC4R, the tan is MC1R, one molecule doing both.',
