    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Ask why there is no clean tanning drug — a peptide that safely, precisely turns
    the skin’s pigment switch and nothing else — and the honest answer begins by
    correcting the question. The potent part is solved. What is missing is
    precision, and precision turns out to be the harder thing to buy.
  </p>

  <h2>Potent is solved. Selective is not.</h2>
  <p>
    There is already an approved drug that switches on the pigment receptor{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> and reliably darkens skin:{' '}
    <a href="/afamelanotide">afamelanotide</a>, a copy of the body’s own{' '}
    <a href="/alpha-msh">α-MSH</a> re-engineered to be far more potent and far
    longer-lasting than the natural hormone. In fair-skinned volunteers it
    measurably increased pigmentation and cut UV damage.<Cite n={5} /> The
    grey-market tanning peptide <a href="/melanotan">Melanotan II</a> is another
    potent one, a cyclic analogue designed in the 1980s to be more stable and more
    active than α-MSH;<Cite n={3} /> the investigational pill{' '}
    <a href="/dersimelagon">dersimelagon</a> is a third. Potency — making the
    receptor fire hard — was never the obstacle.
  </p>
  <p>
    The obstacle is that not one of these hits MC1R <em>alone</em>. Every one of
    them also switches on the other melanocortin receptors, and that is why the
    tan never comes by itself. To see why, you have to look at what these molecules
    are actually recognising.
  </p>

  <h2>One key, five locks</h2>
  <p>
    Every melanocortin — α-MSH, ACTH, the drugs built to imitate them — carries the
    same tiny recognition core: four residues, <strong>His-Phe-Arg-Trp</strong>.
    That short sequence is the working end of the signal, and all five receptors
    evolved to read it.<Cite n={1} /> A small peptide built around that core is,
    in effect, a key cut to a shape that four of the five locks accept. α-MSH
    itself is completely non-selective — it activates MC1R, MC3R, MC4R and MC5R at
    broadly similar strengths.<Cite n={1} /><Cite n={2} />
  </p>

  <Figure
    caption="Every melanocortin ligand carries the same four-residue core, His-Phe-Arg-Trp. Four of the five receptors read it, so one 'key' opens the pigment lock (MC1R) you may be aiming for together with MC4R, MC3R and MC5R. Only MC2R escapes — it answers to the larger ACTH and needs a chaperone protein. Make the key more potent and every lock it fits turns harder: that is potency, not selectivity."
    source="Shared His-Phe-Arg-Trp core and the non-selectivity of α-MSH: Cone 2006; Schiöth 1997. MC2R’s dependence on ACTH and MRAP1: Luo 2023."
  >
    <OneKeyManyLocks />
  </Figure>

  <p>
    The lone exception proves the rule. <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a>, the
    adrenal receptor, does <em>not</em> answer to that four-residue key at all — it
    reads only the longer <a href="/acth">ACTH</a>, and it cannot even reach the
    cell surface without a chaperone protein, MRAP1.<Cite n={10} /> The one
    receptor that escaped the family resemblance did so by needing an entirely
    different key and a helper to use it. The other four are close enough that a
    single molecule reads them all.
  </p>

  <h2>The body never needed a selective one</h2>
  <p>
    This looks like an oversight until you notice how the system actually achieves
    precision. Evolution never made a MC1R-only ligand because it never had to:
    specificity comes from <em>where</em> a peptide is released and{' '}
    <em>which</em> receptor is switched on in that tissue, not from the molecule
    telling the receptors apart.<Cite n={1} /> MC1R sits on pigment cells and
    immune cells; MC4R sits in the appetite circuits of the brain; MC2R sits on the
    adrenal gland. The same broad signal means very different things in each place,
    and the body keeps them straight by geography. There was no selection pressure
    for a molecularly selective key — so there is no natural template for one to
    copy.
  </p>

  <h2>Potency and selectivity are different knobs</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the part that defeats the intuition that a “better” drug would be a
    cleaner one. The classic tricks that make an α-MSH copy powerful — the
    substitutions behind NDP-α-MSH and the cyclic Melanotan analogues — raise
    activity at <em>all</em> the receptors at once, because they strengthen the
    grip on the shared core, not on any one pocket.<Cite n={4} /><Cite n={2} /> So
    afamelanotide is potent <em>and</em> broad; Melanotan II is potent{' '}
    <em>and</em> broad. Turning the dial up does not turn precision on. You can
    watch this spill the other way too: <a href="/setmelanotide">setmelanotide</a>,
    engineered for the appetite receptor MC4R, still grips the pigment receptor
    MC1R almost as tightly, so skin darkening is one of its most common
    effects.<Cite n={7} /> The <a href="/binding-matrix">binding matrix</a> lays out
    those receptor-by-receptor numbers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Selectivity <em>can</em> be coaxed out of these molecules — but the margins are
    exquisite. Take the potent tanning peptide, change a single residue at position
    seven to a bulky unnatural one, and it stops activating MC3R and MC4R and starts
    <em>blocking</em> them — while still switching MC1R on. That is exactly what the
    research compound <a href="/shu9119">SHU9119</a> does.<Cite n={8} /> One atom’s
    worth of change flips the molecule from agonist to antagonist at some receptors
    and not others. It proves subtype discrimination is chemically possible; it also
    shows how narrow the ledge is, and how easily a change aimed at one receptor lands
    on another.
  </p>

  <h2>A selective one exists — for the other reason</h2>
  <p>
    In fact the clean molecule already exists. <strong>BMS-470539</strong> is a
    small-molecule agonist that switches on MC1R and largely spares the rest of the
    family.<Cite n={9} /> But read what it was built for: it was developed to test
    whether firing MC1R could calm inflammation — it was dosed to block cytokine
    release and stop white cells flooding inflamed tissue in mice, and it lives in
    the literature as an <a href="/inflammation">anti-inflammatory</a> tool. Nobody
    took it toward pigmentation. The selectivity that a “clean tanning drug” would
    need is not a chemistry that can’t be done. It is a chemistry that was pointed
    somewhere else, because that is where the medicine was.
  </p>

  <h2>There is no medical reason to build it</h2>
  <p>
    Follow the incentive and the gap explains itself. MC1R agonism for pigment has
    exactly one approved use — <a href="/afamelanotide">erythropoietic
    protoporphyria</a>, a rare disease in which sunlight causes agonising pain — and
    afamelanotide already serves it, delivered as an implant where broad activity is
    tolerated because the photoprotection is worth it.<Cite n={6} /> The enormous
    market is cosmetic tanning, and that is not a road drugs are approved down; the
    grey-market history of <a href="/melanotan">Melanotan II</a> — contaminated
    vials, case reports of changing moles — is a large part of why no serious
    developer wants to plant a flag there.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where the hard medicinal chemistry of melanocortin selectivity <em>is</em> being
    done, MC1R is usually the receptor to <em>avoid</em>. The money is in{' '}
    <a href="/receptors/mc4r">MC4R</a> for obesity, and for those drugs the tan is an
    unwanted side effect — so the newest agents are engineered to <em>spare</em> MC1R,
    not to hit it. The field is spending its selectivity budget teaching drugs to miss
    the pigment receptor. The full landscape of who is building what sits on the{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-agonists">melanocortin agonists</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>Even a perfect one would meet a ceiling</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose someone did build the flawless MC1R-selective peptide. Two ceilings would
    still be waiting. The first is delivery: peptides are digested if swallowed and
    clear quickly if injected, which is why every approved melanocortin peptide is an
    injection or an implant, and why the frontier has moved toward{' '}
    <a href="/oral-peptides">oral small molecules</a> in the first place. The second
    is biology. MC1R agonism only produces pigment if the receptor can answer: the
    common <a href="/genetics">red-hair variants</a> of MC1R are loss-of-function, sitting
    at reduced levels on the cell surface and signalling weakly,<Cite n={11} /> and
    the people who most want a tan are disproportionately the people carrying them.
    You cannot out-dose a receptor that cannot fire, and past a point the melanocyte’s
    own machinery caps how much eumelanin it will make.<Cite n={1} /> A more potent key
    does not linearly make more colour.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is finally changing</h2>
  <p>
    For most of this story the tools did not exist to design a selective melanocortin
    on purpose. That changed in 2020–21, when cryo-electron microscopy resolved the
    receptors at atomic detail — including MC1R with α-MSH and afamelanotide in its
    pocket — and made the near-identical pockets, and the small differences between
    them, finally visible.<Cite n={10} /> Structure-guided redesign has already turned
    that into selectivity: reworking setmelanotide’s core produced a compound with more
    than a hundred-fold preference for MC4R over MC1R — a deliberate, successful attempt
    to separate two receptors that used to come as a pair.<Cite n={12} /> The full
    structural story is on the <a href="/structures">receptor structures</a> page.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the honest state of it. A selective MC1R agonist is no longer a chemistry
    problem the way it was a decade ago — the pictures are in hand and the design methods
    work. What is missing is not the key. It is a reason to cut it: an indication, a
    market, a medicine worth making. Until one appears, “potent” will keep arriving
    without “precise,” and the tan will keep coming with everything else attached.
  </p>

